{"id":129093864771726,"type":"song","name":"First We Take Manhattan","artist":"Leonard Cohen","year":1988,"weight":10,"info":{"img":null},"artist_ref":{"id":204517802555829,"name":"Leonard Cohen","info":{"img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Leonard_Cohen%2C_1988_01.jpg?width=500"}},"labels":[],"genres":[],"lists":[],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"Featured on Leonard Cohen's 1988 breakthrough album *I'm Your Man*, this song demonstrates his extremely low vocal range, notably including a low B flat. Cohen's vocal style, described as more of a drone or \"method acting\" than traditional singing, conveys deep emotion. \"And that record is this song, first we take Manhattan.\"","year":1988,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"The Kings of Quirk","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7_ZmZxnSOnE"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"From his 1988 album *I'm Your Man*, this song is notable for featuring a very low B flat, showcasing the depth of Cohen's distinctive baritone vocal style. \"First we take Manhattan. Listen for the low, low B flat.\"","year":1988,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"The Kings of Quirk","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sEZ13v1LNYQ"}]}],"book_tracks":[],"others":[{"id":150212910820641,"type":"album","name":"Songs of Leonard Cohen","year":1967,"weight":58},{"id":245159512093469,"type":"album","name":"I’m Your Man","year":1988,"weight":38},{"id":116785873150770,"type":"album","name":"Songs of Love and Hate","year":1971,"weight":27},{"id":86241921698950,"type":"album","name":"Songs From a Room","year":1969,"weight":25},{"id":54666725047496,"type":"album","name":"You Want It Darker","year":2016,"weight":18},{"id":218858529690906,"type":"song","name":"Hallelujah","year":null,"weight":16},{"id":270021981975778,"type":"song","name":"Sisters of Mercy","year":null,"weight":13},{"id":122202479342237,"type":"album","name":"Wi